swehouse.c: Koch cusp speeds hold the ascensional difference fixed

Koch trisects the semi-arc of the MC, so a single ascensional difference ad3
serves all four of its own cusps, and it moves as the MC does. The four
AscDash() calls were taken with ad3 held constant, which drops the factor by
which the argument of Asc1() actually advances. The error grows with latitude:

    swetest -b1.1.2000 -ut12:00 -house13.4,60,K -speed

      house 11  330 22 27.4880  1303 52 17.7347  ->   940 42 43.6140
      house 12   33 26 40.9558  1236 35 16.4300  ->  1064 22 41.5880

1303 against a true 940 is 39 per cent high. At latitude 30 it is 465 against
433, at 52.5 it is 736 against 605. Working code reading those speeds will see
them change by that much.

Eight of the twelve change, not two. Cusps 2, 3, 11 and 12 are Koch's own, and
swehouse.c mirrors them onto 8, 9, 5 and 6. At latitude 60 cusp 2 goes
344.7412834 to 392.7506369 and cusp 3 goes 270.5108640 to 345.8546208, so the
two the demonstration does not print move by 14 and 28 per cent.

The reference is a Richardson-extrapolated central difference of each cusp's own
position, the same construction used elsewhere in this set. Against it the worst
relative error over the sample falls from 3.854e-01 to 7.240e-05.

The latitude has to be below the polar circle for any of this to be Koch's own
code. Above it the case bails to Porphyry before reaching the block this patch
changes, and what moves there belongs to the Porphyry patch instead. Testing
Koch at latitude 80 therefore says nothing about this patch, which is worth
saying because it is the natural place to look.

The derivative is a chain through the MC: ad3 = asind(sind(c) * sina) / 3 with
c = atand(tanfi / cosa) and sina = sind(mc) * sine / cosd(fi), differentiated in
closed form and applied as factors (1 -+ k) and (1 -+ 2k) on the four AscDash()
results.

Positions are untouched.

This is one concern lifted out of "swehouse.c: compute house cusp speeds
analytically" so that it can be taken alone. That patch contains this change and
rewrites the surrounding code as well, so the two cannot both be applied; it is
also available as an eight-step series, of which this is one step. Independent of
every other patch in the set.

---
 swehouse.c | 43 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 38 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)
diff --git a/swehouse.c b/swehouse.c
index 668752a..d48d026 100644
--- a/swehouse.c
+++ b/swehouse.c
@@ -1070,7 +1070,7 @@ static int CalcH(
  *  implemented for arguments in degrees.
  ***********************************************************/
 {
-  double tane, tanfi, cosfi, sinfi, tant, sina, cosa, th2;
+  double tane, tanfi, cosfi, sinfi, tant, sina, cosa, th2, mc0;
   double a, c, f, fh1, fh2, xh1, xh2, xs1, xs2, rectasc, ad3, acmc, vemc;
   int 	i, ih, ih2, retc = OK;
   double sine, cose;
@@ -1391,6 +1391,7 @@ static int CalcH(
       strcpy(hsp->serr, "within polar circle, switched to Porphyry"); 
       goto porphyry;
     }
+    mc0 = hsp->mc;
     sina = sind(hsp->mc) * sine / cosd(fi);
     if (sina > 1) sina = 1;
     if (sina < -1) sina = -1;
@@ -1402,10 +1403,42 @@ static int CalcH(
     hsp->cusp[2] = Asc1(th + 120 + ad3, fi, sine, cose);
     hsp->cusp[3] = Asc1(th + 150 + 2 * ad3, fi, sine, cose);
     if (hsp->do_hspeed) {
-      hsp->cusp_speed[11] = AscDash(th + 30 - 2 * ad3, fi, sine, cose);
-      hsp->cusp_speed[12] = AscDash(th + 60 - ad3, fi, sine, cose);
-      hsp->cusp_speed[2] = AscDash(th + 120 + ad3, fi, sine, cose);
-      hsp->cusp_speed[3] = AscDash(th + 150 + 2 * ad3, fi, sine, cose);
+      /* Derivative of the four Asc1() calls just above. If the ad3 or sina
+       * expressions above change, this block has to change with them.
+       * Koch trisects the semi-arc of the MC rather than of each cusp, which
+       * is why one ascensional difference ad3 serves all four and why the
+       * derivative is a single chain through the MC. Like Placidus it is
+       * undefined beyond the polar circles, and the case bails to Porphyry
+       * there before reaching this block.
+       * ad3 is a third of the ascensional difference of the MC, so it moves
+       * as the MC does and the arguments of Asc1() below advance at a rate
+       * different from armc's. AscDash() supplies the derivative with respect
+       * to its first argument, already scaled by ARMCS, so it needs only the
+       * factor by which that argument actually moves.
+       * Differentiating ad3 = asind(sind(c) * sina) / 3 through
+       * c = atand(tanfi / cosa) and sina = sind(mc) * sine / cosd(fi):  */
+      double dad3 = 0, kk;
+      /* Neither test can fail as the code stands: the polar circle bailout
+       * above keeps cos(fi) above sin(ekl), so sina stays strictly inside
+       * +-1, cosa strictly above 0, and uu = sind(c) * sina with it. They
+       * guard the asind() and the division, and cost one comparison. */
+      if (cosa > VERY_SMALL) {
+	double uu = sind(c) * sina;
+	if (fabs(uu) < 1) {
+	  double dsina_dmc = cosd(mc0) * DEGTORAD * sine / cosd(fi);
+	  double dcosa_dsina = -sina / cosa;
+	  double dc_dcosa = (1 / DEGTORAD) * (-tanfi / (cosa * cosa))
+			  / (1 + (tanfi / cosa) * (tanfi / cosa));
+	  double du_dmc = (cosd(c) * DEGTORAD * sina * dc_dcosa * dcosa_dsina
+			   + sind(c)) * dsina_dmc;
+	  dad3 = du_dmc / (3 * DEGTORAD * sqrt(1 - uu * uu)) * hsp->mc_speed;
+	}
+      }
+      kk = dad3 / ARMCS;
+      hsp->cusp_speed[11] = AscDash(th + 30 - 2 * ad3, fi, sine, cose) * (1 - 2 * kk);
+      hsp->cusp_speed[12] = AscDash(th + 60 - ad3, fi, sine, cose) * (1 - kk);
+      hsp->cusp_speed[2] = AscDash(th + 120 + ad3, fi, sine, cose) * (1 + kk);
+      hsp->cusp_speed[3] = AscDash(th + 150 + 2 * ad3, fi, sine, cose) * (1 + 2 * kk);
     }
     break;
   case 'L':	/* Pullen SD sinusoidal delta, ex Neo-Porphyry */
